WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.760
Film Courage: What's your first week working
on a new idea? What are you, if you've gotten

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:09.429
this idea while you're in traffic or wherever
it is standing line at the supermarket but

00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:14.190
then you're actually "Okay i'm gonna invest
time in this." What's it like?

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.289
Long Beach City College Film Professor/Filmmaker,
Elias Daughdrill: It's just notes. I'm very

00:00:18.289 --> 00:00:24.830
disorganized. It's a notebook where I am just
I'm writing either lines of dialogue, ideas

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:32.180
for a scene, something about a character character
breakdown, any number of things that think

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:37.050
are a theme. Something that I want somebody
to say. I don't even know what character would

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:43.700
say it but this is a line that I want something
where it's happening. It's that, it's a bunch

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:51.010
of I don't do the note card thing. I will
write a series of notes. I might even from

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:55.450
those notes like I'll fill up one of those
like those black I don't know if I have any

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:59.180
of those black notebooks you know the things
that in Seven where the guy used to make all

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:02.900
his notes right (one of those). I'll fill
one of those up and I might write a draft

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:08.430
out of that. That draft will be bad but I
have something and then I might then make

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:12.960
an outline from the draft so I've got notes
that I turn into a draft that i will then

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.650
turn into an outline defined structure. I
don't like to structure first because i'm

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:23.360
not as interested in plot. I'm really not
that interested in plot at all. I'm much more

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:29.180
interested in character and theme and so it's
about ideas and how I'm trying to express

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:35.000
those ideas whether it's through characters
actions or their context or whatever and so

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.950
I found that if I tried to impose structure
first which is what I think of as as being

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:46.229
is a structural thing that then I get too
focused on the mechanics of plot which I don't

00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:52.500
care about at all. I want it to be about character
and characters journey however you want to

00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:01.730
describe it so that's why outline actually
comes for me after I've written a draft so

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:06.979
that I can then okay here are all these ideas.
Are there a way I can structure these ideas?

00:02:06.979 --> 00:02:14.870
Okay if that makes sense so it's not an efficient
way like if I was like on for hire for a script

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:19.940
I probably couldn't do it that way but nobody's
knocking on my door to do that paid me a bunch

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:26.290
of money to write a script for them so this
way allows me to to hold on to the things

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:32.200
that I think are more important because if
I'm worried about these kind of story beats

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:37.090
and steps and because American movies are
all about causality (this causes this causes,

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:42.840
this causes this), then I might lose the character
because if the character is doing things that

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:48.349
are contradictory, there's not a clear causality
between them that might be way more interesting

00:02:48.349 --> 00:02:53.790
than if there is. So that's kind of the way
whether that happens over a week. I don't

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:57.170
know. I've got two kids now, who the hell
knows how long it'll take me to get to those

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:02.760
places. It might get me I might get to a draft
in a week but i kind of doubt it. That first

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:08.650
week is probably just going to be notes notes.
Notes and trying to figure out who it is and

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:13.790
then when you actually get a first draft down
are you doing the sort of putting it away

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:21.590
and then looking at it again. That's generally
what i found is the best thing it's. I want

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:26.819
to look at it with fresh eyes. I'm not sending
it to anybody yet because it's not good enough

00:03:26.819 --> 00:03:30.040
yet. You don't want to waste people's time
and I don't want to get a bunch of notes from,

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:33.000
I don't want to waste their time and I also
don't want to get a bunch of notes for stuff

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:40.250
that I'll of course I know what's wrong with
it right after I look at it a second time

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:47.040
so it's don't look at it for whatever a couple
weeks, a month, something like that and then

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:55.480
go back in and look at it and hopefully do
a pretty significant teardown of it. Pieces

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:59.420
of it will survive but it should be a pretty
significant teardown because the first thing

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:04.720
shouldn't be very good. It should...you're
trying to get some ideas out. You're trying

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:10.220
to work around those ideas but it's not going
to be anywhere near what the finished product.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:15.370
I mean that first draft of Faith from 10 years
ago was vastly different than the thing the

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:16.370
movie that we shot.

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:17.659
Film Courage: You still have it?

00:04:17.659 --> 00:04:21.150
Elias: Yeah somewhere it's on a thumb drive
somewhere yeah.

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:23.650
Film Courage: Where did you write it?

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:30.839
Elias: I did the very cliched thing (this
is before I had kids) so I could do this as

00:04:30.839 --> 00:04:39.430
I went up the coast and and just rented a
hotel for like two or three days and banged

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:43.389
out the first draft. I had a whole bunch of
it sort of worked out so it wasn't like a

00:04:43.389 --> 00:04:47.110
what's my idea and three days later i had
a finished draft of the script i had a whole

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:49.840
bunch of notes and stuff that i was working
from but yeah.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:52.749
Film Courage: Did you stay at like an ocean
view hotel?

00:04:52.749 --> 00:04:59.740
Elias: No, I don't have the money to afford
that but it was by the ocean here.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:00.740
Film Courage: Oh it sounds so romantic.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.379
Elias: Yeah I know, I did that but yeah good
luck to me convincing my wife to let me do

00:05:03.379 --> 00:05:07.960
that again now leave her with two kids. So
I don't think that's going to happen.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:09.860
Film Courage: Did you do it with a laptop
or did you write into?

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:16.380
Elias: No yeah I'm too lazy I'm too lazy to
do long form. That was one thing that I had

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:21.789
Final Draft very early on when I was still
an undergrad and I've just gotten used to

00:05:21.789 --> 00:05:23.290
writing that way.

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:29.550
Film Courage: Going back to that 10-year hotel
stay was that ticking clock worth it in terms

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:33.900
of it actually got you to finish it because
you're like "I'm here, I paid for this.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:45.800
Elias: I know it helped but it helped. I'm
sure I can't remember it so long ago but it

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:52.849
was also like it was good to just that was
just the thing i was there to do it was just

00:05:52.849 --> 00:06:01.770
that you know nothing else.It was a good feeling
and also the the idea felt like you have times

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:06.020
when you're reaching for the idea like you're
writing and but you're still reaching for

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:12.710
the idea. The idea was like close enough to
where I could do it in that three days time

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:17.509
you know get it out. There's other scripts
or the idea is not close enough to where I

00:06:17.509 --> 00:06:22.879
wouldn't be able to get it all out in three
days time if that makes any sense?

00:06:22.879 --> 00:06:26.979
Film Courage: It does yeah. That's interesting.
How many drafts of Faith did you write before

00:06:26.979 --> 00:06:29.479
you sent it to your producer Mike?

00:06:29.479 --> 00:06:38.889
Elias: I had probably written three maybe
four something like that i want to say i'd

00:06:38.889 --> 00:06:42.499
have to go back and look but i want to say
it was three or four drafts um and it's a

00:06:42.499 --> 00:06:48.039
yeah like i said it's a vastly different script
than the one um that we ended up shooting

00:06:48.039 --> 00:06:54.009
that thematically it's the same uh structurally
and character-wise it's a little bit different

00:06:54.009 --> 00:06:58.930
so three or four drafts in you send it to
him what's his time frame for getting back

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:06.899
to you what were the notes uh he got back
to me probably within three weeks something

00:07:06.899 --> 00:07:12.690
like that um again i won't make it sound like
i just sent it to him like there was plenty

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:16.800
of people i sent it to who didn't respond
but it was kind of in his wheelhouse like

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:22.279
it's he was he told me when he was like 14
he was thinking about being a priest oh wow

00:07:22.279 --> 00:07:33.189
um but then girls and so then you know girls
happen to be yeah exactly um and um his his

00:07:33.189 --> 00:07:38.469
response i mean he responded the script he
said i like the way it's dealing with these

00:07:38.469 --> 00:07:45.919
kinds of um with issue these kinds of issues
but no judgment to the characters um and then

00:07:45.919 --> 00:07:52.550
he had a few ideas for the ending as i recall
or i don't know so then we we talked on the

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:59.599
phone about it and um he suggested i do another
like scene outline so i did a scene outline

00:07:59.599 --> 00:08:05.219
and then he came out to la because he was
here he's a new york based producer he was

00:08:05.219 --> 00:08:09.369
here because one of his films was i was around
the independent spirit awards i remember that

00:08:09.369 --> 00:08:14.749
and one of his films was nominated for i think
was one of the actors or something and so

00:08:14.749 --> 00:08:18.939
then we got to meet and talk face-to-face
and he suggested a big change i originally

00:08:18.939 --> 00:08:26.419
the story was actually divided between two
characters whose two different stories going

00:08:26.419 --> 00:08:31.419
on and he suggested just to simplify it to
just focus on one character for a number of

00:08:31.419 --> 00:08:38.280
reasons he said don't change the the again
the themes the ideas all that stuff stays

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:44.660
the same you just got to simplify the story
so it's just one person's story so i did that

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:50.520
and he was he responded really positively
to that rewrite and so then we were kind of

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:55.780
off and running in terms of then trying to
generate interest we ended up out i ended

00:08:55.780 --> 00:09:03.350
up out in new york through uh with ifp um
they had a this kind of screenplay what did

00:09:03.350 --> 00:09:09.630
they call it this program that's emergency
emerging storytellers where they selected

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:13.050
it was a pretty competitive program they only
select x number of scripts and then we got

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:20.830
to meet uh with like sony classics and i don't
remember a bunch of different companies you

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:24.810
know trying to generate interest see if you
can get any money we didn't get any money

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:31.500
um but uh but it was a really good thing and
and um and then it was i rewrote a couple

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:39.140
more drafts and then eventually we got there
do you think the themes in faith are ones

